WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.240
Back to the fair.

2
00:00:04.319 --> 00:00:06.842
Stand each of the sixteen prizes

3
00:00:06.921 --> 00:00:08.893
up as a column of blocks

4
00:00:08.893 --> 00:00:11.258
one block for a prize of one

5
00:00:11.258 --> 00:00:13.860
four blocks for a prize of four.

6
00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:17.271
The average prize is the level the columns

7
00:00:17.344 --> 00:00:19.239
reach if we even them out

8
00:00:19.312 --> 00:00:21.571
moving blocks from tall columns

9
00:00:21.644 --> 00:00:22.810
into short ones.

10
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.675
Will that level be above three coins

11
00:00:26.675 --> 00:00:27.625
or below?

12
00:00:27.625 --> 00:00:29.240
Pause and think.

13
00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.340
Keep moving blocks.

14
00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:35.700
Four more moves

15
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.220
and every column has at least three blocks.

16
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:41.300
Two blocks are left over.

17
00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:44.352
Fifty blocks over sixteen columns

18
00:00:44.434 --> 00:00:47.792
is fifty sixteenths: twenty-five eighths

19
00:00:47.792 --> 00:00:50.330
which is three and one eighth.

20
00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:54.283
That level is the expectation of M: the sum

21
00:00:54.283 --> 00:00:55.839
over all outcomes

22
00:00:55.839 --> 00:00:58.788
of the value times its probability.

23
00:00:58.869 --> 00:01:00.917
So the game pays a little

24
00:01:00.999 --> 00:01:02.719
more than the ticket

25
00:01:02.719 --> 00:01:06.240
one eighth of a coin per game, on average.

26
00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:08.781
There is a shorter way.

27
00:01:08.781 --> 00:01:12.021
Group the outcomes by the value of M

28
00:01:12.021 --> 00:01:15.431
one cell pays one, three cells pay two

29
00:01:15.431 --> 00:01:18.500
five pay three, and seven pay four.

30
00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.443
So the expectation is one

31
00:01:21.547 --> 00:01:23.621
times one sixteenth

32
00:01:23.621 --> 00:01:27.044
plus two times three sixteenths

33
00:01:27.044 --> 00:01:30.570
plus three times five sixteenths

34
00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:34.097
plus four times seven sixteenths.

35
00:01:34.097 --> 00:01:36.690
Again, fifty sixteenths.

36
00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.138
In general

37
00:01:38.138 --> 00:01:41.832
E of X is the sum over the values a

38
00:01:41.832 --> 00:01:46.225
of a times the probability that X equals a.

39
00:01:46.225 --> 00:01:49.320
Only the distribution matters.

40
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:53.360
Now a rule that saves a lot of work.

41
00:01:53.456 --> 00:01:56.435
The expectation of a sum is the

42
00:01:56.531 --> 00:01:58.837
sum of the expectations.

43
00:01:58.837 --> 00:02:01.048
The proof is one line

44
00:02:01.048 --> 00:02:05.180
split the sum over outcomes into two sums.

45
00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:08.748
One die has expectation five halves

46
00:02:08.748 --> 00:02:12.771
so the sum of two dice has expectation five

47
00:02:12.771 --> 00:02:15.810
without listing the sixteen sums.

48
00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:18.124
Here is a sharper use.

49
00:02:18.124 --> 00:02:19.464
In every cell

50
00:02:19.553 --> 00:02:22.589
the larger number plus the smaller

51
00:02:22.678 --> 00:02:25.357
number is the sum of the dice.

52
00:02:25.446 --> 00:02:27.679
So E of min is five minus

53
00:02:27.768 --> 00:02:29.554
twenty-five eighths

54
00:02:29.554 --> 00:02:31.875
which is fifteen eighths.

55
00:02:31.875 --> 00:02:35.626
And min and max are far from independent

56
00:02:35.626 --> 00:02:38.840
linearity never needs independence.

57
00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.467
What about a product?

58
00:02:41.467 --> 00:02:44.195
If X and Y are independent

59
00:02:44.195 --> 00:02:47.412
E of X Y is E of X times E of Y.

60
00:02:47.412 --> 00:02:51.505
Group the outcomes by the pair of values

61
00:02:51.602 --> 00:02:54.331
and independence splits each

62
00:02:54.429 --> 00:02:57.060
probability into a product.

63
00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:58.802
For the two dice

64
00:02:58.802 --> 00:03:02.405
the sixteen products add up to one hundred.

65
00:03:02.487 --> 00:03:04.535
One hundred sixteenths is

66
00:03:04.616 --> 00:03:06.336
twenty-five quarters

67
00:03:06.336 --> 00:03:09.530
exactly five halves times five halves.

68
00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:13.966
But drop independence, and the rule fails.

69
00:03:13.966 --> 00:03:16.204
Take X times X itself.

70
00:03:16.204 --> 00:03:20.485
Each row now pays its first number squared

71
00:03:20.485 --> 00:03:23.014
one, four, nine, sixteen.

72
00:03:23.014 --> 00:03:26.030
The average is fifteen halves

73
00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:28.560
not twenty-five quarters.

74
00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:32.671
The expectation is not the whole story.

75
00:03:32.761 --> 00:03:34.934
Compare doubling one die

76
00:03:35.024 --> 00:03:36.925
with adding two dice.

77
00:03:36.925 --> 00:03:39.460
Both have expectation five.

78
00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:43.110
Doubling one die gives two, four

79
00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:47.310
six or eight, each a quarter of the time.

80
00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:50.510
Adding two dice gives the tent

81
00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:53.810
with five the most likely value.

82
00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:56.810
The sum stays closer to five.

83
00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:59.847
The variance measures that spread

84
00:03:59.926 --> 00:04:02.296
the expectation of the squared

85
00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:04.193
distance from the mean.

86
00:04:04.193 --> 00:04:07.353
For two times the first die it is five

87
00:04:07.353 --> 00:04:10.040
and for the sum only five halves.

88
00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:13.294
Computing a variance from

89
00:04:13.401 --> 00:04:15.862
the definition is slow.

90
00:04:15.862 --> 00:04:20.141
Expand the square: X minus mu, squared

91
00:04:20.141 --> 00:04:23.350
is X squared, minus two mu X

92
00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:25.169
plus mu squared.

93
00:04:25.169 --> 00:04:29.020
Take expectations, using linearity.

94
00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:31.612
The middle term becomes

95
00:04:31.709 --> 00:04:33.757
minus two mu squared.

96
00:04:33.757 --> 00:04:35.121
What is left

97
00:04:35.121 --> 00:04:38.241
the variance is E of X squared

98
00:04:38.241 --> 00:04:40.970
minus the square of E of X.

99
00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:45.238
For one die, the squares one, four, nine

100
00:04:45.238 --> 00:04:48.296
sixteen average fifteen halves.

101
00:04:48.296 --> 00:04:52.310
The mean squared is twenty-five quarters.

102
00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:55.464
The difference is five quarters.

103
00:04:55.464 --> 00:04:59.286
And since a variance is never negative

104
00:04:59.286 --> 00:05:03.300
E of X squared is always at least E of X

105
00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:04.160
squared.

106
00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:06.955
Three rules to keep.

107
00:05:06.955 --> 00:05:11.717
Linearity: the expectation of a sum is the

108
00:05:11.828 --> 00:05:15.372
sum of the expectations, always.

109
00:05:15.372 --> 00:05:20.134
Products: E of X Y is E of X times E of Y

110
00:05:20.134 --> 00:05:23.900
but only for independent X and Y.

111
00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:25.825
And the variance

112
00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:29.808
E of X squared minus the square of E of X

113
00:05:29.808 --> 00:05:31.290
never negative.

114
00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:34.353
Try it yourself: for the two dice

115
00:05:34.426 --> 00:05:37.420
find the distribution and the expectation

116
00:05:37.493 --> 00:05:38.808
of the difference

117
00:05:38.808 --> 00:05:41.510
the larger number minus the smaller.

118
00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:44.042
Next lecture leaves probability

119
00:05:44.112 --> 00:05:47.138
for whole numbers: division with remainder

120
00:05:47.138 --> 00:05:49.460
and the greatest common divisor.
